<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Giorgio dei Greci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1539">1539</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint George of the Greeks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Giorgio dei Greci is a church in the Castello district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, built for the city's Greek community and the centre of its Confraternity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Although Venice had close ties to the Byzantine world, the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> rite was long not permitted there; in 1498 the Greeks gained the right to found a confraternity, and in 1539 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> allowed the church to be built, paid for by a tax on all ships from the Orthodox world. Begun by Sante Lombardo, it received a bell tower in 1592 and a dome painted with the Last Judgement. Its treasures include icons by Michael Damaskinos and three that Anna Notaras brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> before 1453.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-giorgio-dei-greci.jpg" alt="San Giorgio dei Greci" width="330" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakub Hałun · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20110722_Venice_San_Giorgio_dei_Greci_4433.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Orthodox Greeks had the church of San Biagio from 1456, the only church where they could celebrate their rites, and in 1514, when it grew overcrowded, were allowed to build <b>San Giorgio dei Greci</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/venice">Social Hierarchy of the Republic of Venice</a></li></ul></section>
